 enforcement to see  where we are in the present moment and  on a mass scale but also to do something  that was previously impossible which is  to find out where we were three weeks  ago three months ago six months ago  before we were even under from  investigation or suspected of a crime  and so Chief Justice Roberts says that  because the technology facilitates a new  form of mass tracking that is not  possible without the technology the  government is required to get a warrant  even if they're merely tracking our  public movements even if they're not  tracking our private movements in our  home even if they're just tracking where  we go on public streets because previous  to the existence of this technology that  type of surveillance was actually not  even feasible so yes mass surveillance  is distinct from individualized targeted  surveillance that law enforcement is  engaged in because they believe that  someone is actually engaged in a crime  new technologies license plate readers  face surveillance technology certainly  cell phone tracking give the government  the power to very cheaply conduct  surveillance of hundreds of millions of  people's movements and activities and  again not just in the present moment but  actually reversing back in time to see  what we've been doing days weeks or  months before so let's now dig in a  little bit to the specifics of how some  of these technologies are being  implemented of different types of  biometric surveillance and facial  recognition where is it currently being  used so um there's a lot we don't know  unfortunately especially about what the  federal government is doing with face  surveillance technology by remote  biometric tracking technologies we view  as distinct from the traditional forms  of biometric surveillance that the  government has conducted including  fingerprints and DNA I mean there isn't  that iris scanning gait scanning so  that's the way that you walk is is also  a biometric which is which just means  something that's unique to each  individual of course our faces there's  even some science that says are the  shape of our ears is a biometric so  these new forms of biometrics that the  FBI has been experimenting with for  years now and that state and local law  enforcement are beginning to use to  track and identify people those are  distinct from fingerprints and DNA for  one simple reason  and we need to think of them as a class  as uniquely dangerous for this reason it  is necessary again this is back to the  concept of friction if law enforcement  wants to get my fingerprints or my DNA  they actually have to send someone  physically to follow me to pick up my  coffee cup or my cigarette but when I'm  thrown it on the ground or in the trash  to test that material that physical  material it is not possible yet maybe it  will be some David it's not possible yet  to pluck someone's DNA from a camera  that is you know coming from a satellite  in space it is easy trivial actually and  it's going to become even easier for  governments and corporations to collect  and store our biometrics  like our iris our face our ears the way  that we walk our gait remotely and  therefore and mass again this concept of  friction is really important you can't  have 1 million police officers  collecting 300 million people's DNA it's  simply impractical you can very easily  in the kind of China style surveillance  system that we're seeing in certain  regions of that country set up camera  networks that facilitate the mass  tracking of people through their  biometrics that are that are detectable  remotely so how is this happening again  we're not sure what's going on at the  federal level the FBI is very secretive  Congress has asked a lot of questions  that have gone on the answer to the of  the FBI  we aim to find out we filed a public  records request in January with against  the FBI and the Drug Enforcement  Administration asking them for  information about how they use these  technologies we're particularly  concerned about in the absence of any  regulation the FBI using these  technologies to track and monitor  dissidents at protests and places like  Standing Rock you know climate racial  justice anti-war anti-trump types of  activists and we do know however that  the FBI has access to 640 million images  of adults in the United States so that's  more obviously than every adult in the  country  essentially every single person with a  state identification card every person  who's been arrested is in a database  that's accessible to the FBI for facial  surveillance purposes that's pretty  terrifying and Congress has thus far  I've done nothing to stop it  we also know that at the local level  like for example here in Massachusetts  the driver's license state ID database  maintained by the Registry of Motor  Vehicles is basically a perpetual lineup  for law enforcement based around  searches so we know that since 2006  state and local and federal law  enforcement have been using that  database to conduct maybe fishing  expeditions pulling images from Facebook  when they want to identify someone and  running it against the arm these  database using facial recognition  technology to try to identify people  that's been happening without any  judicial oversight with no legislative  authorization no probable cause or even  reasonable suspicion is required by  police they don't have to demonstrate  that anybody is involved in a crime  before they can perform one of these  searches and so we're very concerned  that in the absence of any oversight or  accountability at the state level these  systems are being abused and misused  currently yeah I mean my you know any  time when I'm hearing about any policy  and someone presents a very  black-and-white view my instinct is to  be skeptical of that because like with  most things there are sort of things on  both side of the ledger so if we step  back for a moment from the legality I  could imagine 