le government  agencies to type in your name David  Parkman hit enter and then automatically  the software will stitch together a  video showing every single place you've  been over the past five 10 years however  long the video is stored who you were  with what you were doing and prepare a  nice little report David Parkman was  seen outside the cannabis shop on this  date he was seen outside the Planned  Parenthood on this date he was seen at a  mosque on this date he was seen at a  protest on this day he was seen with a  whistle blower on this date right so  that type of surveillance is this stuff  that we're actually the most concerned  about and we think has no place in the  democratic society but it's actually  something that should just be banned  permanently we should never allow  government agencies to create  architectures are sort of surveillance  that facilitate that kind of mass  tracking of people in public space and  has no place in a free society so  there's there's a third way which is  also very scary  which is facial analysis technology so  this is technology that purports to be  able to discern whether someone is happy  or sad or anxious or stressed by  evaluating their face now a lot of its  junk science a really important research  paper was published by scholars at  Northeastern University including dr.  Lisa Behrendt who's a world-renowned  psychologist she reviewed over 2,000  papers in this area and found  essentially that due to cultural  differences you know people with autism  etc depending on the moods that they're  in at different contexts it is actually  not possible to discern how someone is  feeling by evaluating the physical  characteristics of their face so we are  very concerned that law enforcement  other government agencies will start to  adopt technologies that purport to be  able to tell police officers potentially  in a very high-stress dangerous  situation whether the person that  they're looking at is stressed has a  criminal record and you know may be  armed and dangerous and if the  technology gets stuff wrong in that kind  of context which we assume it will  because technology frequently gets  things wrong that could mean the  difference between life and death  for a human being walking on the streets  of an American city and so we want to  make sure again that those types of  facial analysis tools that are already  in relatively widespread use in the  commercial sector so for example in  remote job interviews they're doing  these over they're using these these  technologies over technologies like  Skype when you're applying for a job at  a large corporation we want to make sure  that government agencies are not using  those technologies to evaluate for  example in the interrogation room  whether someone's telling the truth or  not when they're talking to the police  so there are two categories of the  technology that we actually think should  never be adopted in a free society the  other the right like we have an image of  someone's face and we want to identify  this person I think that for example  with dead people potentially with  Alzheimer's patients and potentially in  very very you know specific criminal  investigations where there's a really  high risk to the public like for example  in a murderer  investigation with the appropriate level  of judicial oversight and transparency  and rigorous auditing and and public  oversight that that type of surveillance  may have a place in the 21st century but  there are some forums again that really  just shouldn't and we have the power  again in a democracy to draw that line  and say you know what Amazon and Jeff  Bezos have a certain idea of what the  future will look like we have a  different one and and we're going to  make sure that ours wins out we've been  speaking with Kate Crockford who's the  director of the technology for Liberty  program at the ACLU of Massachusetts  Kate thanks so much for talking to us  about this today I